WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.310
Case Study:

2
00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.787
Chat, Customer, Rider Messaging.

3
00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:08.355
Chapter 41, from the Case Studies part of the System Design Playbook.

4
00:00:10.105 --> 00:00:11.065
At a glance:

5
00:00:11.345 --> 00:00:12.519
Chapter at a glance.

6
00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:13.801
Reader bands:

7
00:00:14.081 --> 00:00:16.385
B1, then B4, Prerequisites:

8
00:00:16.665 --> 00:00:24.815
Ch 03 (SSE versus WebSockets, heartbeats), Ch 14, Ch 20, Ch 40, What you will build:

9
00:00:25.095 --> 00:00:31.217
The order-scoped chat between a customer, the rider delivering their order and, when needed, a support agent.

10
00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:40.308
It includes text and quick replies, delivery photos, read receipts, rider is online, masked calling, and translation between languages.

11
00:00:40.588 --> 00:00:55.009
This is the classic design WhatsApp interview question narrowed to a real product: ordering and delivery guarantees on mobile networks, a multi-node WebSocket tier, presence that doesn't flap, and privacy between strangers.

12
00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:57.721
L1: order, duplicates and gaps.

13
00:00:58.001 --> 00:01:03.847
2,000 simulated conversations ran over lossy mobile networks with a skewed rider clock.

14
00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:17.890
Sorting by the sender's clock showed 59,998 replies before the message they answered, plus 2,407 duplicates and 4,034 missing messages.

15
00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:27.471
Sorting by server time fixed causality but left 539 of a sender's own messages out of order and 4,083 missing.

16
00:01:27.751 --> 00:01:35.453
Client message ids, per-sender order, a per-conversation sequence and gap fetch gave 0 or 0 or 0 or 0.

17
00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:38.463
L2: a real multi-node WebSocket chat.

18
00:01:38.743 --> 00:01:49.645
Two hand-written RFC 6455 WebSocket servers, JDK clients, PostgreSQL for history and Redis pub or sub between nodes.

19
00:01:50.995 --> 00:02:02.003
4,187 sends, including 187 retries of a lost ACK, stored 4,000 messages, and every one was delivered across nodes.

20
00:02:02.283 --> 00:02:10.667
50 riders dropped and reconnected to the other node, and got the 250 messages they missed replayed from PostgreSQL.

21
00:02:10.947 --> 00:02:12.269
Across all phones:

22
00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:15.813
0 duplicates, 0 out of order, 0 gaps.

23
00:02:16.093 --> 00:02:22.323
A virtual thread busy-waiting for its ACK first starved the server, a real bug fixed in the lab.

24
00:02:22.603 --> 00:02:24.629
L3: presence that doesn't flap.

25
00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:27.576
60,000 riders on mobile networks.

26
00:02:28.926 --> 00:02:36.713
Heartbeats every 5 s cost 12,000 writes per second, and riders showed offline 4.5 times an hour when they weren't.

27
00:02:36.993 --> 00:02:42.027
A 20 s grace period after a socket close cut that to 0.55.

28
00:02:42.307 --> 00:02:52.526
Deriving presence from location updates the rider already sends cost 0 extra writes, with real departures shown within 34 s (p95).

29
00:02:52.806 --> 00:02:55.195
L4: keeping phone numbers out of chat.

30
00:02:55.475 --> 00:03:05.459
Of 2,000 messages leaking a contact, a phone-number regex caught 15.8%, and wrongly flagged 100 courier AWB numbers.

31
00:03:06.809 --> 00:03:17.604
Normalising scripts, separators and number words (English and Hindi), with context rules, caught 85.7% with 0 false positives on the known cases.

32
00:03:17.884 --> 00:03:22.065
Numbers split across two messages or spelt with typos still got through.

33
00:03:22.345 --> 00:03:23.327
Reading time:

34
00:03:23.607 --> 00:03:25.399
110 min + code.

35
00:03:26.749 --> 00:03:27.602
Part 1.

36
00:03:27.882 --> 00:03:29.802
Introduction and Why It Matters.

37
00:03:31.052 --> 00:03:33.463
A story: where are you? at the wrong gate.

38
00:03:33.743 --> 00:03:38.265
A customer in a large Bengaluru apartment complex ordered at 8 pm.

39
00:03:38.545 --> 00:03:42.300
The rider reached Gate 2; the customer was waiting at Gate 4.

40
00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:49.535
The first chat design, a REST endpoint plus push notifications, turned a two-minute problem into a twenty-minute one:

41
00:03:49.815 --> 00:03:51.521
Messages appeared out of order.

42
00:03:51.801 --> 00:03:54.873
The rider's cheap phone clock was 90 seconds fast.

43
00:03:55.153 --> 00:04:00.657
His Which gate? appeared after the customer's answer Gate 4, so the customer thought he'd ignored it.

44
00:04:00.937 --> 00:04:02.324
Messages went missing.

45
00:04:02.604 --> 00:04:04.481
A push notification was dropped.

46
00:04:04.761 --> 00:04:08.559
The customer never saw I'm at Gate 2 until they reopened the app.

47
00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:10.890
Duplicates.

48
00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:15.970
On a weak signal, the rider's app retried Coming three times and all three appeared.

49
00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:17.381
Rider offline.

50
00:04:17.661 --> 00:04:24.807
Each time the rider walked through the basement, the customer's screen flipped to Rider is offline and the customer called support.

51
00:04:25.087 --> 00:04:25.983
Privacy.

52
00:04:26.263 --> 00:04:29.719
Frustrated, the rider typed his personal number into the chat.

53
00:04:29.999 --> 00:04:34.991
Two weeks later, the customer reported that the rider was still messaging her on WhatsApp.

54
00:04:36.341 --> 00:04:40.395
The redesigned chat is small in scale compared with a social messenger.

55
00:04:40.675 --> 00:04:47.587
It is scoped to an order, lives for hours, has two or three participants and happens under stress on bad networks.

56
00:04:47.867 --> 00:04:54.800
But it needs the same core guarantees as any messenger, plus privacy rules because the participants are strangers.

57
00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:56.833
Quote:

58
00:04:57.113 --> 00:04:58.777
The thesis of this chapter.

59
00:04:59.057 --> 00:05:06.139
A chat system is a replicated, ordered log per conversation, delivered to phones over unreliable networks.

60
00:05:06.419 --> 00:05:08.019
The design follows from that.

61
00:05:08.299 --> 00:05:20.353
Order and identity come from the server: a client-generated id makes retries idempotent, and a server-assigned per-conversation sequence gives one order and lets every phone detect gaps.

62
00:05:20.633 --> 00:05:24.025
Phone clocks and push delivery are never trusted (L1).

63
00:05:24.305 --> 00:05:34.523
Delivery is push as a hint and sync by sequence as the truth, over a stateless multi-node WebSocket tier whose durable store is the database (L2).

64
00:05:34.803 --> 00:05:39.326
Presence is a derived, debounced signal, not a raw socket state (L3).

65
00:05:39.606 --> 00:05:48.075
And between strangers, privacy is part of the protocol: masked numbers, contact-detail masking and bounded retention (L4).

66
00:05:49.425 --> 00:05:51.729
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

67
00:05:52.009 --> 00:05:53.375
Here is a quick table:

68
00:05:53.655 --> 00:05:55.319
Aspect, and Grocery chat.

69
00:05:55.599 --> 00:05:56.623
Participants:

70
00:05:56.903 --> 00:06:05.223
Customer, assigned rider; support agent joins on escalation; store picker occasionally (your brand is out of stock, substitute?).

71
00:06:05.503 --> 00:06:06.420
Lifetime:

72
00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:15.105
Opens at rider assignment, read-only 2 h after delivery, deleted after 30 days (support cases retained per policy).

73
00:06:15.385 --> 00:06:16.217
Content:

74
00:06:16.497 --> 00:06:24.540
Text, quick replies (At the gate, Call me), photo of delivery spot, location pin, system messages (Rider picked up your order).

75
00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:39.945
Scale: ~1.5M orders per day, ~40% use chat, ~8 messages each about 5M messages per day; evening peak ~400 msg per second; ~60k riders online.

76
00:06:40.225 --> 00:06:41.079
Channels:

77
00:06:41.359 --> 00:06:48.569
In-app WebSocket when foregrounded; push notification (Ch 40) when backgrounded; masked calling as fallback.

78
00:06:49.919 --> 00:06:50.922
Languages:

79
00:06:51.202 --> 00:06:57.623
Rider and customer languages differ (Kannada, Hindi, Tamil, English); optional machine translation.

80
00:06:58.973 --> 00:07:07.805
The customer's order-tracking screen uses SSE (Ch 03); chat is the bidirectional exception that justifies WebSockets.

81
00:07:09.155 --> 00:07:10.478
Learning objectives.

82
00:07:10.758 --> 00:07:12.635
By the end you will be able to:

83
00:07:12.915 --> 00:07:18.675
Guarantee order, uniqueness and completeness of messages on unreliable networks (L1).

84
00:07:18.955 --> 00:07:24.929
Build a multi-node WebSocket tier with durable history, cross-node routing and resume (L2).

85
00:07:25.209 --> 00:07:31.502
Design presence and typing indicators that are honest without being noisy or expensive (L3).

86
00:07:31.782 --> 00:07:38.011
Protect participants' privacy: masked calling, contact-detail masking, retention (L4).

87
00:07:39.361 --> 00:07:46.636
Handle media, receipts, multi-device, translation and moderation, and scale the design up to a general messenger.

88
00:07:47.986 --> 00:07:48.861
Part 2.

89
00:07:49.141 --> 00:07:51.039
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

90
00:07:52.289 --> 00:07:55.276
Intuition: a shared notebook with numbered pages.

91
00:07:55.556 --> 00:07:59.439
Imagine the conversation as a notebook kept at the post office.

92
00:07:59.719 --> 00:08:05.649
Anyone can send in a line; the clerk writes it on the next numbered line and sends copies to everyone.

93
00:08:05.929 --> 00:08:11.412
If your copy says lines 1 to 7 and then 9, you know line 8 is missing and ask for it.

94
00:08:11.692 --> 00:08:17.644
If you send the same line twice because you didn't hear back, the clerk recognises it and doesn't write it again.

95
00:08:17.924 --> 00:08:22.148
Your watch being wrong doesn't matter, because the line numbers decide the order.

96
00:08:23.498 --> 00:08:24.458
Requirements.

97
00:08:24.738 --> 00:08:31.266
Functional. one-on-one (customer, rider) and small-group (plus support) conversations tied to an order.

98
00:08:31.546 --> 00:08:37.306
Send text, quick replies, images and location; system messages from order events.

99
00:08:37.586 --> 00:08:42.109
Delivery states: sent (server stored), delivered (reached the other phone), read.

100
00:08:42.389 --> 00:08:45.183
Online or last-seen and typing indicators.

101
00:08:46.533 --> 00:08:50.736
History on reconnect and on another device (customer's phone and web).

102
00:08:51.016 --> 00:08:56.136
Masked calling; contact-detail masking; report or block; support escalation.

103
00:08:56.416 --> 00:08:57.525
Non-functional.

104
00:08:57.805 --> 00:09:04.333
Message delivery p99 1 s when both apps are foregrounded; push within seconds otherwise.

105
00:09:04.613 --> 00:09:08.411
No loss, no duplicates, one agreed order per conversation.

106
00:09:08.691 --> 00:09:15.773
99.95% availability during delivery hours; graceful degradation to push and calls.

107
00:09:17.123 --> 00:09:22.777
Privacy: no personal numbers exchanged; retention limits; data residency in India.

108
00:09:24.127 --> 00:09:25.172
Estimation.

109
00:09:25.452 --> 00:09:26.817
Here is a quick table:

110
00:09:27.097 --> 00:09:28.527
Quantity, and Estimate.

111
00:09:28.807 --> 00:09:37.660
Messages: ~5M per day; peak ~400 per second (evening), 3x headroom ~1,200 per second.

112
00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:48.201
Concurrent connections: ~60k riders (long-lived) + ~150k customers with open chat at peak about 200k WebSockets.

113
00:09:48.481 --> 00:09:58.764
Connection nodes: ~20 to 50k connections per node is comfortable on the JVM with virtual threads or Netty, then 6 to 10 nodes with headroom.

114
00:09:59.044 --> 00:10:10.095
Storage: ~300 B per message, then ~1.5 GB per day; 30-day retention about 45 GB (plus images in object storage).

115
00:10:10.375 --> 00:10:11.207
Presence:

116
00:10:11.487 --> 00:10:21.001
60k riders; heartbeats every 30 s = 2,000 writes per second, or zero extra if derived from location updates (L3).

117
00:10:22.351 --> 00:10:29.583
The traffic is modest; the difficulty is correctness on bad networks and long-lived connections across many nodes.

118
00:10:30.933 --> 00:10:32.939
Order, identity and completeness.

119
00:10:33.219 --> 00:10:35.181
Three identifiers do all the work:

120
00:10:35.461 --> 00:10:40.923
Client message id (UUID generated on the phone): makes retries idempotent.

121
00:10:41.203 --> 00:10:48.243
The server stores (conversation, sender, clientmsgid) uniquely and returns the original sequence for a retry.

122
00:10:48.523 --> 00:10:58.699
Sender sequence (per sender, per conversation): lets the server keep each person's own messages in the order they wrote them, even if the network reorders their requests.

123
00:11:00.049 --> 00:11:09.585
Conversation sequence (assigned by the server on commit): the single order everyone displays, and the basis of sync, give me everything after 47.

124
00:11:09.865 --> 00:11:18.910
L1 compared designs over 2,000 simulated conversations with lossy links and a rider clock skewed up to ±2 minutes:

125
00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:21.625
Here is a quick table:

126
00:11:21.905 --> 00:11:28.284
