151
00:12:10.271 --> 00:12:11.017
0.

152
00:12:11.297 --> 00:12:12.812
Own messages out of order:

153
00:12:13.092 --> 00:12:13.839
0.

154
00:12:15.189 --> 00:12:17.471
Phone clocks can't order a conversation.

155
00:12:17.751 --> 00:12:20.439
Replies appeared before the questions they answered.

156
00:12:20.719 --> 00:12:30.383
Server time fixes causality between people, because a reply is sent after its question was stored, but not a sender's own burst, which the network reorders.

157
00:12:30.663 --> 00:12:38.194
Push delivery is a hint: dropped pushes silently lose messages unless the phone compares sequences and fetches gaps.

158
00:12:38.474 --> 00:12:46.815
Show a pending message immediately with a local sending state (optimistic UI), then place it at its sequence when the ACK arrives.

159
00:12:48.165 --> 00:12:49.403
The WebSocket tier.

160
00:12:49.683 --> 00:12:53.757
A connection lives on one node; the other participant may be on another.

161
00:12:54.037 --> 00:12:56.149
L2 built this with two real nodes:

162
00:12:56.429 --> 00:12:57.176
Connect:

163
00:12:57.456 --> 00:13:08.571
HELLO(user, conversation, lastSeq); the node authenticates (short-lived token), registers the session locally, and replays everything after lastSeq from the database.

164
00:13:08.851 --> 00:13:24.701
Send: the node stores the message in one transaction, increment the conversation's lastseq (update... returning), insert with the unique client id, then ACKs the sender with the sequence, and publishes to a pub or sub channel for the conversation.

165
00:13:26.051 --> 00:13:31.235
Fan-out: every node subscribed to the conversation delivers to its local sessions.

166
00:13:31.515 --> 00:13:36.251
The receiving phone checks the sequence: duplicate, then drop; gap, then fetch.

167
00:13:36.531 --> 00:13:40.371
Reconnect anywhere: nodes are stateless apart from live sockets.

168
00:13:40.651 --> 00:13:46.817
L2's riders reconnected to the other node and received the 250 messages they had missed.

169
00:13:47.097 --> 00:13:48.291
L2's results:

170
00:13:48.571 --> 00:14:06.449
4,000 messages across nodes; 187 duplicate retries absorbed; 0 duplicates, 0 gaps and 0 reordering on any phone; p50 ~93 ms end to end on a 2-CPU sandbox with durable commits (timings vary).

171
00:14:07.799 --> 00:14:09.847
Pub or sub is fire-and-forget.

172
00:14:10.127 --> 00:14:17.764
A message published while a node restarts is lost from the live path, but the database has it, and the phone's gap check fetches it.

173
00:14:18.044 --> 00:14:21.735
That's why the durable log, not the broker, defines completeness.

174
00:14:22.015 --> 00:14:23.999
Routing options at larger scale:

175
00:14:24.279 --> 00:14:31.105
Broadcast pub or sub (Redis pattern subscribe, as in L2): simple; every node sees every message.

176
00:14:31.385 --> 00:14:33.945
Fine for thousands of msg per second.

177
00:14:34.225 --> 00:14:41.564
Channel per conversation, subscribed only by nodes with a participant online: less traffic, more subscription churn.

178
00:14:42.914 --> 00:14:50.615
User, then node registry plus direct node-to-node forwarding (gRPC): targeted; needs the registry kept fresh.

179
00:14:50.895 --> 00:15:02.309
Kafka partitioned by conversation into the chat service, and a connection tier that subscribes by user, the WhatsApp or Slack-style separation of message store or router and gateway.

180
00:15:03.659 --> 00:15:21.920
Connection management: heartbeats (ping or pong) under every idle timeout on the path (Ch 03); a bounded connection lifetime; draining on deploy (tell clients to reconnect elsewhere, with jitter); per-connection send buffers with limits, so slow consumers don't consume memory.

181
00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.376
And never busy-wait on a virtual thread:

182
00:15:24.656 --> 00:15:33.723
L2's first version spun while waiting for ACKs, pinned the sandbox's two carrier threads and stalled every connection (Ch 19).

183
00:15:35.073 --> 00:15:37.227
Receipts, typing and multi-device.

184
00:15:37.507 --> 00:15:39.939
Sent = the ACK with a sequence.

185
00:15:40.219 --> 00:15:44.358
Delivered = the receiving phone acknowledges up to sequence N.

186
00:15:44.638 --> 00:15:49.033
Read = the receiving phone reports its read watermark, read up to N.

187
00:15:49.313 --> 00:15:55.094
Store watermarks per participant, not per message: one row per participant per conversation.

188
00:15:55.374 --> 00:16:04.377
Typing indicators are ephemeral: pub or sub only, never stored, throttled (at most one every 3 s), and they expire automatically.

189
00:16:04.657 --> 00:16:16.347
Multi-device: each device keeps its own lastSeq; a user's own sent messages are echoed to their other devices (L2's phones ignore their own echoes but advance lastSeq).

190
00:16:17.697 --> 00:16:18.529
Presence.

191
00:16:18.809 --> 00:16:23.545
Mobile connections drop constantly: lifts, basements, tower handovers.

192
00:16:23.825 --> 00:16:27.985
Half of them drop silently, with no TCP close reaching the server.

193
