224
00:17:54.392 --> 00:18:01.752
Grace periods (debouncing) absorb flapping at the cost of ~20 s detection latency for real departures.

225
00:18:02.032 --> 00:18:10.885
Derive presence from traffic you already have: riders send location every 10 s for tracking (Ch 47), which is a free heartbeat.

226
00:18:11.165 --> 00:18:19.848
Show last seen 2 min ago instead of a hard online or offline, and don't make business decisions (reassigning an order) on one missed heartbeat.

227
00:18:21.198 --> 00:18:33.145
Store presence in a TTL cache (Redis SET key EX 35); fan presence changes out only to interested parties (the customer's chat and dispatch), not to everyone.

228
00:18:34.495 --> 00:18:36.969
Media, location and system messages.

229
00:18:37.249 --> 00:18:46.572
Images (delivery spot photos) upload directly to object storage with a pre-signed URL; the chat message carries a reference and thumbnail.

230
00:18:46.852 --> 00:18:53.380
Scan uploads, strip EXIF location metadata unless it's intended, and expire with the conversation.

231
00:18:53.660 --> 00:19:00.423
Location pins are messages with coordinates; live location sharing uses the tracking stream, not chat.

232
00:19:00.703 --> 00:19:10.537
System messages (Rider picked up your order, Order delivered) come from order events and get sequence numbers like any other message, so the timeline is coherent.

233
00:19:11.887 --> 00:19:14.298
Privacy and safety between strangers.

234
00:19:14.578 --> 00:19:22.770
Masked calling: calls go through a virtual number that maps to the pair only during the order window, so nobody needs a personal number.

235
00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:28.959
Contact-detail masking: people still type numbers into chat, often deliberately disguised.

236
00:19:29.239 --> 00:19:37.453
L4 measured detectors on 2,000 leaking messages and 18,000 ordinary ones, including 300 hard negatives:

237
00:19:38.803 --> 00:19:40.168
Here is a quick table:

238
00:19:40.448 --> 00:19:43.072
Detector, and Recall, and False positives.

239
00:19:43.352 --> 00:19:45.144
Regex for a 10-digit mobile.

240
00:19:45.424 --> 00:19:46.235
Recall:

241
00:19:46.515 --> 00:19:48.115
15.8%.

242
00:19:48.395 --> 00:19:49.589
False positives:

243
00:19:49.869 --> 00:19:52.515
100 (courier AWB numbers).

244
00:19:52.795 --> 00:19:58.789
Normalise separators, Devanagari digits, +91 or 0; UPI or email.

245
00:19:59.069 --> 00:19:59.880
Recall:

246
00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:02.016
59.0%.

247
00:20:02.296 --> 00:20:03.491
False positives:

248
00:20:03.771 --> 00:20:09.573
200 (AWBs, our own support email). + any-script digits, number words, context rules.

249
00:20:09.853 --> 00:20:10.664
Recall:

250
00:20:10.944 --> 00:20:12.779
85.7%.

251
00:20:13.059 --> 00:20:14.253
False positives:

252
00:20:14.533 --> 00:20:16.517
0 on the known hard negatives.

253
00:20:17.867 --> 00:20:25.590
Plain numbers are rare in real leaks: people write them with spaces, words or another script because plain numbers get caught.

254
00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:34.446
Normalisation is what makes detection work, and context rules keep it from masking an AWB, an OTP or the support address.

255
00:20:34.726 --> 00:20:40.294
What no per-message rule catches is a number split across messages or deliberately misspelt.

256
00:20:40.574 --> 00:20:44.563
That needs conversation-level detection, and it will never be perfect.

257
00:20:44.843 --> 00:20:48.129
Mask, don't block; log for trust and safety review.

258
00:20:48.409 --> 00:20:54.254
Report and block, visible to support; automatic closure of the conversation after delivery.

259
00:20:55.604 --> 00:21:06.548
Retention: read-only 2 h after delivery, deleted after 30 days unless attached to a support case; images deleted with the conversation (Ch 36).

260
00:21:06.828 --> 00:21:14.273
Moderation for abuse and harassment: keyword and classifier flags, human review, rider or customer sanctions.

261
00:21:15.623 --> 00:21:16.669
Translation.

262
00:21:16.949 --> 00:21:19.658
Riders often don't read the customer's language.

263
00:21:19.938 --> 00:21:22.455
Quick replies are pre-translated templates.

264
00:21:22.735 --> 00:21:27.450
Free text can be machine-translated on display, with the original shown on tap.

265
00:21:27.730 --> 00:21:36.114
Store the original, cache translations per message and language, and never translate numbers or addresses in a way that changes them.

266
00:21:37.464 --> 00:21:38.339
Storage.

267
00:21:38.619 --> 00:21:40.219
Here is the SQL code.

268
00:21:43.069 --> 00:21:53.885
Partition by day and drop partitions for retention. update conversations set lastseq = lastseq + 1... returning serialises writes per conversation.

269
00:21:54.165 --> 00:22:01.653
That's fine for two or three participants; a huge group chat would need a different sequencer, such as a partition leader in Kafka.

270
00:22:01.933 --> 00:22:14.413
At messenger scale, a wide-column store keyed by (conversation, seq) (Cassandra, ScyllaDB, HBase, as Discord and Facebook have used) handles the write volume (Ch 06).

271
00:22:15.763 --> 00:22:17.747
Scaling up to a general messenger.

272
00:22:18.027 --> 00:22:22.592
If the interviewer asks for WhatsApp rather than order chat, the same core stretches:

273
00:22:22.872 --> 00:22:29.165
Group chats with thousands of members: fan-out on read for large groups, fan-out on write for small ones.

274
00:22:29.445 --> 00:22:39.899
End-to-end encryption (Signal protocol): the server stores ciphertext and routes; features like translation and contact masking move to the client or disappear.

275
00:22:40.179 --> 00:22:48.541
Offline delivery queues per device, with the server deleting messages once all devices have acknowledged them (WhatsApp's original model).

276
00:22:49.891 --> 00:22:56.249
Connection tier of millions of sockets per cluster (Erlang or Elixir, Netty), with a session registry.

277
00:22:56.529 --> 00:23:00.817
Global routing with regional stores and message relay between regions.

278
00:23:02.167 --> 00:23:03.297
Interview framing.

279
00:23:03.577 --> 00:23:10.447
Lead with the per-conversation sequence and client message id: they solve ordering, dedupe and sync in one idea.

280
00:23:10.727 --> 00:23:16.337
Then the connection tier: stateless nodes, pub or sub or registry, replay on reconnect.

281
00:23:16.617 --> 00:23:26.964
Then presence (debounced, derived), receipts (watermarks), media (pre-signed uploads), and, for this domain, privacy (masked calling, contact masking, retention).

282
00:23:27.244 --> 00:23:31.169
Mention push as the background path via the notification system.

