60,000 riders on shift; ~6 brief drops or hour each (median 8 s, half silent); real. offline ~0.5 or hour. design extra writes false offline fan-out real offline shown. per second per. rider-hr. events per second after. p50 or p95.

438
00:37:44.666 --> 00:37:54.757
S1) heartbeat 5 s, timeout 10 s, instant on close 12,000 4.53 239.2 0 s or 10 s.

439
00:37:55.037 --> 00:38:06.215
S2) heartbeat 30 s, timeout 60 s, instant on close 2,000 3.06 165.7 0 s or 57 s.

440
00:38:06.495 --> 00:38:15.498
S3) S2 + 20 s grace after a close 2,000 0.55 40.4 20 s or 57 s.

441
00:38:15.778 --> 00:38:26.530
S4) from location updates (10 s), timeout 35 s, 20 s grace 0 0.67 46.1 20 s or 34 s.

442
00:38:26.810 --> 00:38:28.325
The rest is in the chapter.

443
00:38:31.175 --> 00:38:32.028
Reading it:

444
00:38:32.308 --> 00:38:39.711
S1: fast detection, but 12,000 writes per second and 4.5 false offline flips per rider-hour.

445
00:38:39.991 --> 00:38:47.351
S2 saves writes but still flags 3.06 false offlines an hour, because it trusts every socket close.

446
00:38:47.631 --> 00:38:56.548
S3: a 20 s grace after a close cuts false offlines to 0.55, at the cost of 20 s to show a real departure.

447
00:38:57.898 --> 00:39:00.821
S4: presence derived from the location stream:

448
00:39:01.101 --> 00:39:09.762
0 extra writes, 0.67 false flips per rider-hour, and real departures shown within 34 s at p95.

449
00:39:10.042 --> 00:39:15.738
That's faster than S2 and S3, whose silent-drop detection waits for a 60 s timeout.

450
00:39:17.088 --> 00:39:19.477
L4, Keeping phone numbers out of the chat.

451
00:39:19.757 --> 00:39:20.696
Requirement:

452
00:39:20.976 --> 00:39:31.344
Mask contact details people type into chat, including disguised ones, without masking OTPs, order ids, AWB numbers or our support address.

453
00:39:31.624 --> 00:39:34.504
A synthetic corpus of 20,000 messages.

454
00:39:34.784 --> 00:39:37.771
The 2,000 leaks are written 14 different ways.

455
00:39:38.051 --> 00:39:48.504
The 18,000 ordinary messages include 300 hard negatives: courier AWB numbers, our support email, and number words in normal speech.

456
00:39:49.854 --> 00:39:53.203
Here is the Java code, ContactLeakL4.java.

457
00:39:53.483 --> 00:39:57.835
It runs to 166 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

458
00:39:58.115 --> 00:40:06.371
ContactLeakL4.java, run with: java ContactLeakL4.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

459
00:40:06.651 --> 00:40:08.571
KEEPING PHONE NUMBERS OUT OF THE CHAT.

460
00:40:08.851 --> 00:40:16.297
Customers and riders talk through the platform so that neither sees the other's. real number (calls go through masked numbers).

461
00:40:16.577 --> 00:40:20.587
People still type numbers into chat, to take a tip off-platform, to.

462
00:40:23.437 --> 00:40:24.355
On screen:

463
00:40:24.635 --> 00:40:26.171
20,000 messages:

464
00:40:26.451 --> 00:40:31.805
18,000 ordinary (with OTPs, order ids, flats, amounts, pincodes).

465
00:40:32.085 --> 00:40:36.245
2,000 leaking a contact. detector recall false positives.

466
00:40:36.525 --> 00:40:51.907
D1) regex for a 10-digit mobile number 15.8% 100 for example, Courier AWB 7169564695 for. the return.

467
00:40:52.187 --> 00:41:02.917
D2) normalise, then regex + UPI or email 59.0% 200 for example, Write to support@grocery.in. for the refund.

468
00:41:03.197 --> 00:41:09.469
D3) + any-script digits, number words, context rules 85.7% 0.

469
00:41:09.749 --> 00:41:19.947
Recall by how the number was written (D1, then D3): +91 100%, then 100%. dashed 0%, then 100%.

470
00:41:20.227 --> 00:41:21.741
The rest is in the chapter.

471
00:41:24.591 --> 00:41:25.445
Reading it:

472
00:41:25.725 --> 00:41:27.026
D1 (regex):

473
00:41:27.306 --> 00:41:36.266
15.8% recall, since only plain and +91 numbers match, and it masks 100 courier AWB numbers.

474
00:41:36.546 --> 00:41:46.487
D2 (normalise): separators, Devanagari digits, +91 or 0, UPI and email lift recall to 59.0%.

475
00:41:46.767 --> 00:41:52.378
It also masks our own support email and the AWBs (200 false positives).

476
00:41:53.728 --> 00:42:05.973
D3: digits in any script via Character.getNumericValue (so Kannada works), number words in English and Hindi, and context rules for AWBs, OTPs and our own domain.

477
00:42:06.253 --> 00:42:11.928
Recall 85.7%, 0 false positives on these known hard negatives.

478
00:42:12.208 --> 00:42:14.128
Real traffic will produce new ones.

479
00:42:14.408 --> 00:42:20.637
What got through: numbers split across two messages (0%) and misspelt number words (3%).

480
00:42:20.917 --> 00:42:26.421
Catching those needs conversation-level detection, and people will keep inventing new forms.

481
00:42:26.701 --> 00:42:31.565
Masked calling, which removes the need to share a number, matters more than the detector.

482
00:42:32.915 --> 00:42:33.854
Part 6.

483
00:42:34.134 --> 00:42:35.414
Production Concerns.

484
00:42:36.664 --> 00:42:37.816
Failure modes.

485
00:42:38.096 --> 00:42:39.461
Here is a quick table:

486
00:42:39.741 --> 00:42:42.024
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

487
00:42:42.304 --> 00:42:43.669
Client-clock ordering.

488
00:42:43.949 --> 00:42:44.760
Symptom:

489
00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:47.237
Replies before questions (L1).

490
00:42:47.517 --> 00:42:48.520
Mitigation:

491
00:42:48.800 --> 00:42:50.891
Server-assigned conversation sequence.

492
00:42:51.171 --> 00:42:53.069
Retries after lost ACK.

493
00:42:53.349 --> 00:42:54.160
Symptom:

494
00:42:54.440 --> 00:42:56.296
Duplicates (L1, L2).

495
00:42:56.576 --> 00:42:57.579
Mitigation:

496
00:42:57.859 --> 00:42:59.587
Unique client message id.

497
00:42:59.867 --> 00:43:01.979
Dropped pushes, pub or sub loss.

498
00:43:02.259 --> 00:43:03.069
Symptom:

499
00:43:03.349 --> 00:43:05.120
Missing messages (L1).

500
00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:06.403
Mitigation:

501
00:43:06.683 --> 00:43:08.773
Gap detection and fetch by sequence.

502
00:43:09.053 --> 00:43:10.120
Node restart.

503
00:43:10.400 --> 00:43:11.211
Symptom:

504
00:43:11.491 --> 00:43:12.579
Sockets drop.

505
00:43:12.859 --> 00:43:13.861
Mitigation:

506
00:43:14.141 --> 00:43:17.107
Reconnect anywhere with lastSeq (L2), jittered.

507
00:43:17.387 --> 00:43:19.221
Busy-waiting on virtual threads.

508
00:43:19.501 --> 00:43:20.312
Symptom:

509
00:43:20.592 --> 00:43:23.088
Whole node stalls (L2's first run).

510
00:43:23.368 --> 00:43:24.371
Mitigation:

511
00:43:24.651 --> 00:43:26.933
Never spin; block or use futures.

512
00:43:27.213 --> 00:43:28.365
Presence flapping.

513
00:43:28.645 --> 00:43:29.456
Symptom:

514
00:43:29.736 --> 00:43:31.315
False offline (L3).

515
00:43:31.595 --> 00:43:32.597
Mitigation:

516
00:43:32.877 --> 00:43:35.544
Grace periods, derived presence, last seen.

517
00:43:36.894 --> 00:43:38.025
Contact sharing.

518
00:43:38.305 --> 00:43:39.115
Symptom:

519
00:43:39.395 --> 00:43:42.019
Off-platform contact, harassment (L4).

520
00:43:42.299 --> 00:43:43.302
Mitigation:

521
00:43:43.582 --> 00:43:46.206
Masked calling, masking, report or block.

522
00:43:46.486 --> 00:43:47.787
Hot conversation.

523
00:43:48.067 --> 00:43:48.878
Symptom:

524
00:43:49.158 --> 00:43:50.971
One row serialises writes.

525
00:43:51.251 --> 00:43:52.254
Mitigation:

526
00:43:52.534 --> 00:43:55.691
Fine for one-on-one; different sequencer for big groups.

527
00:43:57.041 --> 00:43:58.129
Observability.

528
00:43:58.409 --> 00:44:04.489
Messages per second, end-to-end latency (send, then delivered watermark), ACK latency.

529
00:44:04.769 --> 00:44:10.423
Gap fetches per 1,000 messages (a rising rate means push or pub or sub trouble).

530
00:44:10.703 --> 00:44:14.031
Connections per node, reconnect rate, handshake failures.

531
00:44:14.311 --> 00:44:19.388
Presence flips per rider-hour; masking hits by style and false-positive reports.

532
00:44:20.738 --> 00:44:21.677
Security.

533
00:44:21.957 --> 00:44:29.125
Short-lived connection tokens bound to user and order; authorise every HELLO against the conversation's participants.

534
00:44:29.405 --> 00:44:34.589
Validate message size and kinds; rate-limit sends per user (Ch 39).

535
00:44:34.869 --> 00:44:38.538
Pre-signed uploads scoped to the conversation; content scanning.

536
00:44:38.818 --> 00:44:43.938
Encryption in transit; sensitive originals encrypted at rest with short retention.

537
00:44:45.288 --> 00:44:46.184
Performance.

538
00:44:46.464 --> 00:44:52.245
Gateways on Netty or virtual threads; small per-connection buffers; batching writes per socket.

539
00:44:52.525 --> 00:44:54.445
Coalesce typing and receipt events.

540
00:44:54.725 --> 00:44:57.776
Replay in pages; clients cache history locally.

541
00:44:59.126 --> 00:44:59.851
Cost.

542
00:45:00.131 --> 00:45:06.233
Long-lived connections dominate compute; drop sockets when the app backgrounds and rely on push.

543
00:45:06.513 --> 00:45:12.934
Masked-calling minutes and translation calls are per-use costs: cache translations, offer quick replies.

544
00:45:13.214 --> 00:45:15.987
Retention bounds storage (Ch 35).

545
00:45:17.337 --> 00:45:18.276
Part 7.

546
00:45:18.556 --> 00:45:20.263
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

547
00:45:21.513 --> 00:45:22.430
Watch out:

548
00:45:22.710 --> 00:45:23.350
1.

549
00:45:23.630 --> 00:45:25.379
Ordering by client timestamps.

550
00:45:25.659 --> 00:45:27.515
Phone clocks are wrong (L1:

551
00:45:27.795 --> 00:45:32.510
59,998 replies before their questions).

552
00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:34.777
Watch out:

553
00:45:35.057 --> 00:45:35.761
2.

554
00:45:36.041 --> 00:45:37.599
No client message id.

555
00:45:37.879 --> 00:45:41.569
Every retry after a lost ACK becomes a duplicate.

556
00:45:42.919 --> 00:45:43.837
Watch out:

557
00:45:44.117 --> 00:45:44.863
3.

558
00:45:45.143 --> 00:45:47.874
Trusting push or pub or sub for completeness.

559
00:45:48.154 --> 00:45:51.546
Both drop messages; the sequence and the store are the truth.

560
00:45:52.896 --> 00:45:53.813
Watch out:

561
00:45:54.093 --> 00:45:54.797
4.

562
00:45:55.077 --> 00:45:56.891
Sticky state in gateway nodes.

563
00:45:57.171 --> 00:46:00.051
Reconnecting to another node must work (L2).

564
00:46:01.401 --> 00:46:02.318
Watch out:

565
00:46:02.598 --> 00:46:03.366
5.

566
00:46:03.646 --> 00:46:05.502
Instant offline on socket close.

567
00:46:05.782 --> 00:46:08.342
Mobile sockets flap; debounce (L3).

568
00:46:09.692 --> 00:46:10.609
Watch out:

569
00:46:10.889 --> 00:46:11.636
6.

570
00:46:11.916 --> 00:46:13.708
Per-message read receipts rows.

571
00:46:13.988 --> 00:46:16.036
Watermarks per participant are enough.

572
00:46:17.386 --> 00:46:18.303
Watch out:

573
00:46:18.583 --> 00:46:19.330
7.

574
00:46:19.610 --> 00:46:21.274
Storing typing indicators.

575
00:46:21.554 --> 00:46:24.135
Ephemeral events belong on pub or sub only.

576
00:46:25.485 --> 00:46:26.403
Watch out:

577
00:46:26.683 --> 00:46:27.365
8.

578
00:46:27.645 --> 00:46:29.181
Exposing phone numbers.

579
00:46:29.461 --> 00:46:32.960
Use masked calling and contact masking between strangers.

580
00:46:34.310 --> 00:46:35.227
Watch out:

581
00:46:35.507 --> 00:46:36.254
9.

582
00:46:36.534 --> 00:46:38.795
A single phone-number regex for masking.

583
00:46:39.075 --> 00:46:44.153
Catches 16% of real leaks and flags AWB numbers (L4).

584
00:46:45.503 --> 00:46:46.420
Watch out:

585
00:46:46.700 --> 00:46:47.404
10.

586
00:46:47.684 --> 00:46:49.092
Chats that never close.

587
00:46:49.372 --> 00:46:51.847
Scope to the order; enforce retention.

588
00:46:53.197 --> 00:46:54.114
Watch out:

589
00:46:54.394 --> 00:46:55.226
11.

590
00:46:55.506 --> 00:46:57.341
Busy-waiting on virtual threads.

591
00:46:57.621 --> 00:47:00.522
It pins carrier threads and stalls the node (L2).

592
00:47:01.872 --> 00:47:02.789
Watch out:

593
00:47:03.069 --> 00:47:03.859
12.

594
00:47:04.139 --> 00:47:06.336
Uploading media through the chat socket.

595
00:47:06.616 --> 00:47:09.027
Use pre-signed object-storage uploads.

596
00:47:10.377 --> 00:47:11.251
Part 8.

597
00:47:11.531 --> 00:47:12.875
Interview Q and A.

598
00:47:14.125 --> 00:47:14.957
Beginner.

599
00:47:15.237 --> 00:47:16.261
Question 1.

600
00:47:16.541 --> 00:47:20.936
Design a chat between a customer and a delivery rider, the main components?

601
00:47:21.216 --> 00:47:22.581
Thirty-second answer:

602
00:47:22.861 --> 00:47:30.989
Mobile apps with WebSocket connections to stateless chat gateways; a chat service that assigns each conversation's sequence numbers,

603
00:47:31.269 --> 00:47:49.829
dedupes by client message id and stores messages; pub or sub (or a registry) to route messages to the gateway holding the other participant; push notifications when an app is backgrounded; presence in a TTL cache; object storage for photos; masked calling; retention.

604
00:47:51.179 --> 00:47:52.182
Question 2.

605
00:47:52.462 --> 00:47:54.830
How do you keep messages in the right order?

606
00:47:55.110 --> 00:47:56.475
Thirty-second answer:

607
00:47:56.755 --> 00:48:05.587
The server assigns a monotonically increasing sequence number per conversation when it stores each message, and every client displays by that sequence.

608
00:48:05.867 --> 00:48:07.617
Client clocks are unreliable.

609
00:48:07.897 --> 00:48:12.505
Each sender's own order is kept with a per-sender sequence the server enforces.

610
00:48:13.855 --> 00:48:14.900
Question 3.

611
00:48:15.180 --> 00:48:18.039
What happens if the rider's phone retries a message?

612
00:48:18.319 --> 00:48:19.684
Thirty-second answer:

613
00:48:19.964 --> 00:48:32.081
The retry carries the same client-generated message id; the server finds it (unique constraint) and returns the original sequence in the ACK, so nothing is stored or shown twice.

614
00:48:33.431 --> 00:48:34.498
Question 4.

615
00:48:34.778 --> 00:48:36.378
How do read receipts work?

616
00:48:36.658 --> 00:48:38.023
Thirty-second answer:

617
00:48:38.303 --> 00:48:43.871
Each participant's phone reports watermarks: delivered up to seq N and read up to seq M.

618
00:48:44.151 --> 00:48:54.093
The server stores one row per participant per conversation and forwards changes to the other participants, who render ticks for messages at or below the watermark.

619
00:48:55.443 --> 00:48:56.445
Intermediate.

620
00:48:56.725 --> 00:48:57.835
Question 5.

621
00:48:58.115 --> 00:49:01.784
How do messages reach a user connected to a different server?

622
00:49:02.064 --> 00:49:03.429
Thirty-second answer:

623
00:49:03.709 --> 00:49:13.523
After storing a message, the chat service publishes it to a pub or sub channel for the conversation (or looks up the recipient's node in a registry and forwards it).

624
00:49:13.803 --> 00:49:16.597
The node holding the recipient's socket delivers it.

625
00:49:16.877 --> 00:49:20.653
If the recipient isn't connected, a push notification goes out.

626
00:49:20.933 --> 00:49:23.600
On reconnect the phone syncs from its last sequence.

627
00:49:24.950 --> 00:49:26.059
Question 6.

628
00:49:26.339 --> 00:49:28.750
How does a phone catch up after being offline?

629
00:49:29.030 --> 00:49:30.395
Thirty-second answer:

630
00:49:30.675 --> 00:49:39.721
It reconnects to any node with HELLO(lastSeq); the node replays stored messages after that sequence in pages, then live messages continue.

631
00:49:40.001 --> 00:49:44.630
Gaps detected later (a jump in sequence) trigger a fetch of the missing range.

632
00:49:45.980 --> 00:49:47.068
Question 7.

633
00:49:47.348 --> 00:49:50.228
How do you implement presence without flooding the system?

634
00:49:50.508 --> 00:49:51.873
Thirty-second answer:

635
00:49:52.153 --> 00:50:08.879
Keep presence as a TTL key refreshed by heartbeats or, better, by traffic the client already sends (location updates); debounce socket closes with a grace period; publish changes only to interested parties; show last seen rather than a hard offline.

636
00:50:10.229 --> 00:50:11.253
Question 8.

637
00:50:11.533 --> 00:50:13.367
How do you send photos in chat?

638
00:50:13.647 --> 00:50:15.013
Thirty-second answer:

639
00:50:15.293 --> 00:50:26.535
The app requests a pre-signed upload URL scoped to the conversation, uploads directly to object storage, then sends a chat message referencing the object (with a thumbnail).

640
00:50:26.815 --> 00:50:33.130
The service scans the image, strips unintended metadata, and deletes it when the conversation expires.

641
00:50:34.480 --> 00:50:35.547
Question 9.

642
00:50:35.827 --> 00:50:39.133
Why WebSockets here when order tracking uses SSE?

643
00:50:39.413 --> 00:50:40.779
Thirty-second answer:

644
00:50:41.059 --> 00:50:47.693
Tracking is server, then client only, so SSE's plain HTTP, auto-reconnect and resume fit.

645
00:50:47.973 --> 00:50:58.341
Chat is bidirectional and interactive, sends, receipts, typing, so a single full-duplex connection avoids a request per message and gives lower latency.

646
00:50:59.691 --> 00:51:00.459
Senior.

647
00:51:00.739 --> 00:51:01.785
Question 10.

648
00:51:02.065 --> 00:51:06.267
Walk through what makes delivery exactly-once from the user's point of view.

649
00:51:06.547 --> 00:51:07.913
Thirty-second answer:

650
00:51:08.193 --> 00:51:25.835
Client message ids make sends idempotent at the server; the server assigns one sequence per stored message; delivery to phones is at-least-once (push, pub or sub, replay) and phones dedupe by sequence; gaps in the sequence trigger fetches, so nothing is silently lost.

651
00:51:26.115 --> 00:51:29.849
Result: each message appears once, in order, on every device.

652
00:51:31.199 --> 00:51:32.329
Question 11.

653
00:51:32.609 --> 00:51:36.385
How do you deploy chat gateways without disconnecting everyone at once?

654
00:51:36.665 --> 00:51:38.031
Thirty-second answer:

655
00:51:38.311 --> 00:51:49.383
Drain: stop accepting new connections on the old node, send clients a reconnect control message with a jittered delay, let them reconnect elsewhere with lastSeq, then terminate.

656
00:51:49.663 --> 00:51:53.247
Bound connection lifetime so load rebalances naturally.

657
00:51:53.527 --> 00:51:56.193
Keep gateways stateless so reconnects are cheap.

658
00:51:57.543 --> 00:51:58.631
Question 12.

659
00:51:58.911 --> 00:52:03.029
How do you stop riders and customers exchanging personal phone numbers?

660
00:52:03.309 --> 00:52:04.674
Thirty-second answer:

661
00:52:04.954 --> 00:52:28.271
Make it unnecessary, masked calling for the order window, and mask contact details in chat: normalise text (separators, digits in any script, number words in the languages used), detect numbers or UPI or emails, apply context rules so OTPs, order ids and AWBs aren't masked, look across consecutive messages, and log for trust and safety.

662
00:52:28.551 --> 00:52:30.194
Close chats after delivery.

663
00:52:31.544 --> 00:52:32.824
Question 13.

664
00:52:33.104 --> 00:52:35.024
How would you add machine translation?

665
00:52:35.304 --> 00:52:36.669
Thirty-second answer:

666
00:52:36.949 --> 00:52:53.056
Store the original; translate on display into the reader's language, cached per message and language; show translated, see original; pre-translate quick replies; protect numbers, addresses and names from translation; handle failures by showing the original.

667
00:52:54.406 --> 00:52:55.601
Question 14.

668
00:52:55.881 --> 00:52:59.443
What storage would you use, and how does it change at 100x?

669
00:52:59.723 --> 00:53:01.089
Thirty-second answer:

670
00:53:01.369 --> 00:53:13.273
Today, PostgreSQL partitioned by day with (convid, seq) keys and a per-conversation sequence via update... returning, ~5M messages per day, 30-day retention.

671
00:53:13.553 --> 00:53:25.990
At 100x, a wide-column store keyed by conversation and sequence (Cassandra, ScyllaDB), a sequencer per conversation partition (Kafka partition or leader), and history paging.

672
00:53:27.340 --> 00:53:28.236
Architect.

673
00:53:28.516 --> 00:53:29.711
Question 15.

674
00:53:29.991 --> 00:53:33.724
Design WhatsApp-scale messaging, what changes from order chat?

675
00:53:34.004 --> 00:53:35.369
Thirty-second answer:

676
00:53:35.649 --> 00:53:57.388
Millions of concurrent connections per region (Erlang or Netty gateways, session registry); per-device offline queues deleted after acknowledgement; group chats with fan-out strategies by size; end-to-end encryption, so the server routes ciphertext; media via CDN; multi-region routing; and far larger storage with a wide-column store.

677
00:53:57.668 --> 00:54:01.487
The core, client ids, sequences, sync by watermark, stays.

678
00:54:02.837 --> 00:54:04.031
Question 16.

679
00:54:04.311 --> 00:54:07.191
How does chat degrade when parts of the system fail?

680
00:54:07.471 --> 00:54:08.837
Thirty-second answer:

681
00:54:09.117 --> 00:54:16.349
Pub or sub down: messages are still stored and ACKed; delivery falls back to push and gap-fetch on open.

682
00:54:16.629 --> 00:54:24.031
Database down: sends fail fast with retry, the queue stays on the phone, and masked calling is promoted in the UI.

683
00:54:24.311 --> 00:54:27.234
Gateway node loss: clients reconnect elsewhere.

684
00:54:27.514 --> 00:54:30.778
Push provider down: in-app sync still works when opened.

685
00:54:32.128 --> 00:54:33.472
Question 17.

686
00:54:33.752 --> 00:54:36.525
How do you balance safety moderation with privacy?

687
00:54:36.805 --> 00:54:38.171
Thirty-second answer:

688
00:54:38.451 --> 00:54:56.819
Scope data to purpose: automated masking and abuse detection in-line, human review only on reports or strong signals, originals of masked content retained briefly and encrypted, strict access controls and audit for trust and safety staff, clear user policies, and retention limits.

689
00:54:57.099 --> 00:55:01.877
With E2E encryption, moderation shifts to client-side and user reports.

690
00:55:03.227 --> 00:55:04.401
Question 18.

691
00:55:04.681 --> 00:55:05.939
Build or buy chat?

692
00:55:06.219 --> 00:55:07.585
Thirty-second answer:

693
00:55:07.865 --> 00:55:14.158
Chat SDK vendors (Sendbird, Stream and others) offer channels, receipts and moderation quickly.

694
00:55:14.438 --> 00:55:29.286
Order chat is small and tightly integrated with orders, masked calling, rider apps and privacy rules, and data residency matters, a strong case for building a focused service, possibly using a vendor for masked calling and translation.

695
00:55:30.636 --> 00:55:31.873
Question 19.

696
00:55:32.153 --> 00:55:34.436
How would you test the chat system's correctness?

697
00:55:34.716 --> 00:55:36.081
Thirty-second answer:

698
00:55:36.361 --> 00:55:59.572
Property-based and simulation tests like L1 (lossy, reordering, duplicating networks; skewed clocks) asserting no duplicates, gaps or reorders; integration tests like L2 with real gateways, database and pub or sub including node kills and reconnects; chaos tests dropping pub or sub and pushes; load tests of connection counts and reconnect storms.

699
00:56:00.922 --> 00:56:02.031
Question 20.

700
00:56:02.311 --> 00:56:05.042
What metrics tell you chat is working for customers?

701
00:56:05.322 --> 00:56:06.687
Thirty-second answer:

702
00:56:06.967 --> 00:56:23.650
Delivery latency (send, then delivered watermark), gap-fetch rate, reconnect success, presence flip rate, masking hits and false-positive reports, and outcome metrics: delivery success at first attempt, support contacts per order, and couldn't find the rider complaints.

703
00:56:23.930 --> 00:56:26.853
Chat exists to make handovers smooth; measure that.

704
00:56:28.203 --> 00:56:29.099
Part 9.

705
00:56:29.379 --> 00:56:30.787
Summary and Practice.

706
00:56:32.037 --> 00:56:33.125
Key takeaways.

707
00:56:33.405 --> 00:56:40.167
A conversation is an ordered log: server-assigned sequence, client message ids, per-sender order (L1).

708
00:56:40.447 --> 00:56:47.509
Push and pub or sub are hints; the store is the truth: gap detection and replay by sequence (L1, L2).

709
00:56:47.789 --> 00:56:51.757
Stateless gateways: reconnect to any node with lastSeq (L2).

710
00:56:52.037 --> 00:56:55.749
Presence is derived and debounced; show last seen (L3).

711
00:56:56.029 --> 00:56:58.802
Receipts are watermarks; typing is ephemeral.

712
00:57:00.152 --> 00:57:06.211
Privacy between strangers: masked calling, normalised contact masking, retention (L4).

713
00:57:06.491 --> 00:57:09.115
Never busy-wait on virtual threads (L2).

714
00:57:10.465 --> 00:57:11.787
Decision cheat sheet.

715
00:57:12.067 --> 00:57:13.433
Here is a quick table:

716
00:57:13.713 --> 00:57:15.035
Question, and Answer.

717
00:57:15.315 --> 00:57:16.254
Transport:

718
00:57:16.534 --> 00:57:19.137
WebSocket in foreground; push in background.

719
00:57:19.417 --> 00:57:20.099
Order:

720
00:57:20.379 --> 00:57:22.513
Per-conversation sequence from the store.

721
00:57:22.793 --> 00:57:23.582
Dedupe:

722
00:57:23.862 --> 00:57:26.486
Unique (conversation, sender, clientmsgid).

723
00:57:26.766 --> 00:57:27.982
Cross-node routing:

724
00:57:28.262 --> 00:57:32.315
Pub or sub by conversation, then registry or Kafka at scale.

725
00:57:32.595 --> 00:57:33.406
Catch-up:

726
00:57:33.686 --> 00:57:36.353
HELLO(lastSeq) replay + gap fetch.

727
00:57:36.633 --> 00:57:37.465
Presence:

728
00:57:37.745 --> 00:57:41.755
TTL key from existing traffic, 20 s grace, last seen.

729
00:57:42.035 --> 00:57:42.867
Media:

730
00:57:43.147 --> 00:57:45.238
Pre-signed object-storage uploads.

731
00:57:45.518 --> 00:57:46.435
Privacy:

732
00:57:46.715 --> 00:57:49.958
Masked calling, contact masking, 30-day retention.

733
00:57:51.308 --> 00:57:52.439
Exercises.

734
